"Every program so far in this course followed rules that a person wrote. The robot turned left because someone wrote “turn left.” But some jobs are very hard to write rules for. How would you write the rule for telling an apple from an orange in a photo, or for reading messy handwriting? There are too many cases to list. For jobs like these, there is another way.": "a9930bb2c50f",
"Every program so far in this course followed rules that a person wrote. The robot turned left because someone wrote “turn left.” But some jobs are very hard to write rules for. How would you write the rule for telling an apple from an orange in a photo, or for reading messy handwriting? There are too many cases to list. For jobs like these, there is another way. Instead of rules, give the computer examples. Each example comes with the right answer: this one is an apple, that one is an orange. Picture the fruits lying on a table, placed by how heavy and how red they are, and an upright wall across the table. The wall is the computer’s rule: apples on one side, oranges on the other. At the start the wall is in a bad place. Now the computer looks at one example. If the wall already has that fruit on the correct side, nothing changes. If the fruit is on the wrong side, the computer moves the wall a little, toward getting that one right. Then it looks at the next example. In our model there are 24 fruits. After looking at 40 examples, the wall has moved 5 times, and every fruit is on the correct side. Nobody told the computer where the wall should go. This is called machine learning, and the steps of looking and adjusting are called training. Notice that the computer is still doing exactly what a program says, as in Lesson 9. The program just does not say “this is an apple.” It says “when you are wrong, adjust a little.” That is why the word “learn” is in quotation marks. The computer is adjusting numbers. It does not understand what a fruit is. Because everything comes from the examples, the examples matter a great deal. Give the computer only four, and it is satisfied too soon: the wall fits those four, but it gets some new fruits wrong. Give it examples with wrong answers, and the wall never settles, because no wall can agree with all of them. The computer cannot tell which answers to doubt. Real systems do not use one wall. They use neural networks, with a great many numbers to adjust, and they are used to recognize handwriting, faces, and speech. What such a network knows is spread through those numbers. It is not written out anywhere in the program. For a large network, it can be extremely difficult to follow exactly how an answer was reached. So people test it on new cases it has never seen, and they keep checking what it says.": "fe063c3e57dd",
